1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:01,597
Why does ice float?

2
00:00:01,897 --> 00:00:04,428
Most solids sink in their own liquid.

3
00:00:04,428 --> 00:00:06,549
Frozen water does the opposite.

4
00:00:07,049 --> 00:00:12,812
Water molecules are tiny V shapes that cling to each other with hydrogen bonds.

5
00:00:13,112 --> 00:00:20,179
In liquid water, those bonds break and re-form all the time, so the molecules can crowd close.

6
00:00:20,679 --> 00:00:26,949
Freeze it, and each molecule locks onto four neighbours, in an open, six-sided pattern.

7
00:00:27,249 --> 00:00:29,890
That pattern holds more empty space.

8
00:00:29,890 --> 00:00:35,099
Ice takes up about 9% more room than the water, so it floats.

9
00:00:35,849 --> 00:00:41,352
Lucky for fish: lakes freeze from the top, and the ice shields the water below.

10
00:00:41,652 --> 00:00:46,816
Ice floats because freezing locks water's molecules into a roomier pattern.
